  <h2 id="vlver">What a verification engineer actually does</h2>

  <p>
    Verification is about proving a design works across many situations, not only the obvious one. Engineers write tests, run them and study why some fail.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Activity</th>
          <th>Example</th>
          <th>Output</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Activity"><strong>Plan tests</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Example">List normal, edge and error cases</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Test plan</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Activity"><strong>Write a testbench</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Create inputs and expected outputs</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Testbench code</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Activity"><strong>Run and debug</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Study waveforms when a test fails</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Bug report</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Activity"><strong>Measure coverage</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Check which cases were never tried</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Coverage summary</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Learners who enjoy puzzle solving often prefer verification to design.
  </p>

